C.append(['title', 'Why and Where We Lose New Logo Deals: An Analysis of Closed-Lost Deals'])
C.append(['meta', 'Author: Will Ritchings, Director of Revenue Operations  ·  Audience: Findigs Leadership Team and GTM Leadership Team  ·  Date: October 1, 2026'])
C.append(['meta', 'Data: 845 closed New Logo deals (74 Closed Won, 771 Closed Lost), close dates October 1, 2025 – September 30, 2026, HubSpot portal 243635806'])

h1('Introduction')
p('This document explains why and where Findigs loses New Logo deals, identifies the root causes behind those losses, and proposes goals, owners, and a plan to raise our win rate. Over the last twelve months we closed 845 New Logo deals and won 74 of them, a win rate of 8.8%. That rate has fallen in each of the last four quarters, from 15.9% in Q4 2025 to 2.6% in Q3 2026. We are asking leadership to make four decisions in this meeting: approve the six goals and their targets, approve the five strategic priorities and their owners, commit to a decision on our Mid-Market motion by November 15, 2026, and approve the CRM changes in Priority 5 that make our loss data trustworthy enough to manage against.')
p('The central finding is that we do not lose deals at the end of the sales process; we lose them at the start. Seventy percent of all closed deals are lost before a business case is built, while deals that reach Negotiation & Execution win 49% of the time. The causes are upstream of the closing conversation: first meetings that never happen or never convert, Discovery calls that end with "not now" or "too expensive," and a Mid-Market segment that we sell the same way we sell SMB and almost never win. All exhibits referenced below are in Appendix A. Methodology and data-quality notes are in Appendix B, and answers to the questions we expect from this room are in Appendix C.')

h1('Goals')
p('Each goal is measured on New Logo deals closed between January 1 and June 30, 2027, against the trailing twelve-month baseline in this document unless stated otherwise. Goals 2 and 4 are the levers; Goal 1 is the outcome they produce. If Intro-to-Discovery conversion reaches 70% and Discovery-to-Business-Case advancement reaches 60% while downstream conversion holds, the win rate rises from 8.8% to 12.4% (8.8% × 70.0/60.1 × 60.0/49.6).')
b('**Raise the New Logo win rate.** Win rate is Closed Won divided by all closed deals, by count. Baseline 8.8% (74 of 845); target 12.0%; change +3.2 points (+36%). Interim checkpoint: Q4 2026 win rate at or above 8.8%, which halts the decline from Q3 2026 (2.6%).')
b('**Raise Intro & Challenge to Discovery / Demo conversion.** The share of closed deals that reach Discovery or a later stage. Baseline 60.1% (508 of 845); target 70.0%; change +9.9 points. This cuts Intro-stage exits from 39.9% of closed deals to 30% or less.')
b('**Cut disengagement losses.** The share of closed-lost deals coded Unresponsive, Demo didn\'t happen, or DQ\'d account. Baseline 46.6% (88 of 189 losses in Q3 2026, the first full quarter with all three picklist values); target 30% or less; change −16.6 points.')
b('**Raise Discovery / Demo to Business Case advancement.** The share of deals reaching Discovery that advance to Business Case or later, or win. Baseline 49.6% (252 of 508); target 60.0%; change +10.4 points.')
b('**Decide and then fix Mid-Market.** Leadership decides the Mid-Market (3,000–19,999 units) motion by November 15, 2026. If we keep the segment, Mid-Market deals reaching Negotiation & Execution rise from 7.5% (10 of 134) to 15.0% (+7.5 points) and the segment win rate rises from 1.5% (2 of 134) to 6.0% or more.')
b('**Make loss data trustworthy.** Two measures. The share of closed-lost deals with a competitor or incumbent recorded rises from 18.5% in Q3 2026 (35 of 189, either competitor field, including an explicit "None") to 90%. The share of deals past Intro whose amount was updated from the create-time list-rate calculation rises from 21% (106 of 508) to 75%.')

h1('Tenets')
p('**We fix the funnel where it leaks most first.** Seventy percent of closed deals are lost before a business case exists, so that is where we spend effort. Negotiation, where we already win half the time, is not the first priority. **We count deals, not list-price dollars, until dollars mean something.** The deal amount is set at creation as units multiplied by a list rate, and 79% of deals past Intro still carry that number, so dollar-weighted metrics today mostly measure unit counts. **A metric built on a broken field is worse than no metric.** An earlier draft of this analysis concluded that two SDRs drove a 3.9% versus 41% win-rate gap; the gap came from reading the wrong ownership field on migrated records and disappeared when the field was corrected. **Qualification is revenue work.** A deal disqualified at Intro costs an AE minutes; a dead deal that sits in Discovery for a month costs forecast accuracy and selling time. **We manage leading indicators weekly.** Show rate, Intro-to-Discovery conversion, and Discovery-to-Business-Case advancement move within weeks; win rate lags by a quarter.')

h1('State of the Business')
p('**Volume grew; wins did not.** Closed New Logo volume rose from 126 deals in Q4 2025 to 240 in Q1 2026 and 285 in Q2 2026, while wins stayed flat at 20, 24, and 25 (Exhibit 2). The win-rate decline from 15.9% to 8.8% over those three quarters came from more losses, not fewer wins. In Q3 2026 the pattern broke: wins fell to 5 from 194 closes, a 2.6% win rate, against 10.6% in the prior nine months (two-proportion test, p = 0.001). Among deals that reached Discovery, the Q3 win rate was 4.9% against 17.0% before (p = 0.002). Deals created after the July 14, 2026 HubSpot migration have produced 1 win in 86 closes. Monthly detail is in Exhibit 3.')
p('**Most deals exit before a business case.** Of 845 closed deals, 337 (39.9%) were lost from Intro & Challenge, 256 (30.3%) from Discovery / Demo, 103 (12.2%) from Business Case & Stakeholder Alignment, 71 (8.4%) from Negotiation & Execution, and 4 (0.5%) from Trial; 74 (8.8%) were won (Exhibit 1). Sixty-seven of the 74 wins passed through Negotiation & Execution, and 49.3% of deals that reached Negotiation won. Our closing motion works; the deals that reach it are too few.')
p('**Each funnel tier loses for different reasons.** We group stages into three tiers: top of funnel (TOFU) is Intro & Challenge; middle of funnel (MOFU) is Discovery / Demo and Business Case; bottom of funnel (BOFU) is Negotiation & Execution and Trial. At TOFU (337 losses), the top reasons are Unresponsive (124, 36.8%), Other (70, 20.8%), and Demo didn\'t happen (48, 14.2%). At MOFU (359 losses), they are Bad Timing (95, 26.5%), Unresponsive (83, 23.1%), and Price Conscious (71, 19.8%). At BOFU (75 losses), Price Conscious leads (18, 24.0%), followed by Unresponsive (17, 22.7%) (Exhibit 4).')
p('**The loss mix is shifting toward disengagement.** Comparing the first six months of the window (October 2025 – March 2026) with the last six (April – September 2026), total losses rose 39% (322 to 449). Disengagement reasons together rose from 28.0% to 42.8% of losses, while Price Conscious fell from 18.6% to 9.4% and Other fell from 19.6% to 15.6% (Exhibit 6). The trailing three-month view in Exhibit 5 shows the same movement: Demo didn\'t happen reached 25% of losses by September and Bad Timing rose from 15% to 24% between June and September, while Price Conscious fell from 19% in March to 6% in September. Part of this shift is recoding, because the Demo didn\'t happen and DQ\'d account values were only added to the picklist in July 2026.')
p('**Mid-Market converts at a fraction of SMB.** SMB deals (under 3,000 units, 695 deals) win 10.1%; Mid-Market deals (3,000–19,999 units, 134 deals) win 1.5%, with 2 wins in twelve months (p = 0.001). Both segments reach Discovery at the same rate (60.4% and 59.7%), but only 7.5% of Mid-Market deals reach Negotiation, against 19.4% for SMB (Exhibit 7). Won Mid-Market deals took a median of 130 days to close, against 27 days for SMB. Mid-Market accounts for 55% of the list-rate value of deals lost after Intro ($30.2M of $54.6M). Enterprise (20,000 units or more) had 12 closed deals and 2 wins, which is too few to conclude from.')
p('**Source matters more than salesperson.** Deals sourced from the data-provider channel won 0 of 53. Outbound SDR deals, 54% of all closed deals, won 8.1%; organic-search inbound won 14.3% and direct inbound won 12.0% (Exhibit 9). By contrast, win rates do not differ significantly across the eight AEs with 30 or more deals (chi-square, p = 0.69) or across the eight SDRs with 30 or more deals (p = 0.87). What does differ by AE is how many deals reach Discovery (p = 0.009): Ian McLaughlin moved 49.5% of his deals to Discovery against 63.3% for everyone else.')
p('**Dead deals linger.** Deals that eventually advance spend a median of 2 to 8 days in each stage. Deals that eventually die spend a median of 27 days in Intro, 31 in Discovery, 40 in Business Case, and 53 in Negotiation before anyone closes them out (Exhibit 8). That inflates the open pipeline that our forecast relies on.')

h1('Lessons Learned')
p('**Lesson 1: We book meetings that do not happen or do not convert, and until July we could not see it.** Four in ten closed deals never get past the first stage, and 72% of those losses are Unresponsive, Other, or Demo didn\'t happen. The root cause is that our booking process optimizes for meetings held, not meetings that matter. There is no required confirmation sequence, no standard reschedule process, and no booking criteria tied to confirmed pain or role. The data-provider channel shows the extreme case: 53 deals and no wins. The problem was hidden by our own instrumentation. Before July 2026 the loss picklist had no value for a no-show, so no-shows were recorded as Unresponsive or Other; notes on 74 lost deals describe a demo that never happened under a different reason code. Now that we can see it, disengagement is our largest and fastest-growing loss family. Confidence is high on the size of the problem and medium on the root cause, which we will confirm through SDR call reviews in Priority 1.')
p('**Lesson 2: Discovery ends without urgency, and price arrives before value.** Half of the deals that reach Discovery are lost there. The two leading reasons, Bad Timing and Price Conscious, both point to Discovery calls that do not establish a compelling event or quantify the cost of the prospect\'s problem. MEDDPICC, our declared sales methodology, requires that Discovery identify pain and metrics before the deal advances, but we do not enforce those exit criteria. Price is also being anchored too early: 71 of 102 price losses occur in MOFU, and 46 of those happen at Discovery itself, before any business case exists. The falling share of price losses is encouraging but unexplained. It may reflect a real change, or reps may be recording price objections as Bad Timing, whose share rose over the same months. We will test that with Gong call reviews rather than assume it.')
p('**Lesson 3: We sell Mid-Market as if it were large SMB.** Mid-Market prospects engage at the same rate as SMB prospects and then stall. Their deals need more stakeholders, a longer cycle (130 days versus 27 for the deals we won), and a business case that survives a buying committee. Our coverage model, stage expectations, and offer are the same as for SMB. The result is 132 losses and 2 wins a year in the segment that holds the majority of lost list-rate value. Confidence is high on the outcome and medium on the cause, because two wins are too few to show what works.')
p('**Lesson 4: Volume grew faster than quality, and Q3 broke the pattern.** From Q4 2025 to Q2 2026 we more than doubled closed volume without adding wins, which means the added volume was lower quality. That is consistent with Lessons 1 and 2. Q3 2026 is a separate break that we cannot yet explain: wins fell from 25 to 5 in one quarter. The leading hypotheses are disruption from the July migration (1 win from 86 post-migration deals), bulk close-outs of stale deals around cutover, a change in pricing or packaging, and a new SDR cohort whose 49 deals produced 1 win, though that sample is too small to judge. Excluding open deals does not explain the drop. Even if all 9 open deals at Negotiation or Trial were to win, the Q3 win rate would be 6.9%. We will not set FY27 quota assumptions until Priority 4 explains Q3.')
p('**Lesson 5: Our loss data under-reports the truth.** This analysis surfaced five gaps that each distorted a conclusion before we corrected for it:')
b('SDR ownership is split across two fields that agree on only 10% of pre-migration records.')
b('Competitors are named on 139 lost deals but coded as the loss reason on only 38. The most-named competitors, AppFolio (51) and Yardi (32), are property management platforms with bundled screening, which suggests platform-bundled screening is a larger competitive force than our reason codes show.')
b('Deal amount is a list-rate calculation on most deals.')
b('The loss picklist changed mid-year.')
b('Stage dates are frequently updated in batches: advancing deals move through stages in 2–8 days, which is faster than a buying process moves.')
p('Each of these gaps is fixable in the CRM, and Priority 5 fixes them.')
p('**What is not the problem.** BOFU execution is not the constraint: only 9.7% of losses happen there and half of Negotiation-stage deals win. Individual AE and SDR win rates are statistically indistinguishable once ownership is read correctly. We do not propose rep-level performance actions on win rate. Coaching attention belongs on Discovery conversion, where AEs do differ.')

h1('Strategic Priorities')
p('**Priority 1: Show-rate and booking-quality program.** Owner: Dan Soltis (SDR leadership), with Marketing Operations; live by November 15, 2026.')
p('*Prediction:* Intro-to-Discovery conversion rises from 60.1% to 70% and disengagement losses fall from 46.6% to 30% or less of losses (Goals 2 and 3).')
p('*Plan:*')
b('Booking criteria: confirmed pain, role, and unit count recorded before a meeting is booked.')
b('Confirmation cadence: touches at 48 hours, 24 hours, and 2 hours before the meeting.')
b('Reschedules: a no-show triggers a task to reschedule within five business days.')
b('Data-provider channel: paused from November 1 until its lists are filtered against our ideal customer profile.')
b('Weekly reporting: show rate and Intro-to-Discovery conversion by SDR and source, added to the weekly GTM dashboard.')

p('**Priority 2: Discovery that earns the business case.** Owner: Phil Aroian (Head of Sales), with Revenue Operations for enablement. Exit criteria live by November 30, 2026; ROI model by December 15, 2026.')
p('*Prediction:* Discovery-to-Business-Case advancement rises from 49.6% to 60% (Goal 4), and MOFU losses coded Bad Timing or Price Conscious fall as a share of losses.')
p('*Plan:*')
b('Exit criteria: MEDDPICC Discovery requirements (identified pain, quantified metrics, and a dated compelling event) recorded before a deal can move to Business Case.')
b('Pricing: moved from the first demo to Business Case, where it is presented with an ROI model built on the prospect\'s eviction and fraud-loss exposure.')
b('Gong trackers: switched on for price and timing language on first calls.')
b('Call reviews: 20 Discovery-stage losses reviewed each month.')
b('AE coaching: targeted coaching for AEs below 55% Discovery reach.')

p('**Priority 3: Mid-Market decision.** Owner: Brian Korchin (President and CRO), with Sales and Product; decision by November 15, 2026.')
p('*Prediction:* A clear decision either produces a Mid-Market motion that doubles Negotiation reach to 15%, or it removes roughly 130 low-yield deals a year from SDR targeting and the FY27 quota model (Goal 5).')
p('*Plan:* A 30-day review has three inputs:')
b('Interviews with lost Mid-Market prospects drawn from the 70 who reached Discovery but not Negotiation.')
b('A teardown of the 2 wins.')
b('A side-by-side of three options: dedicated Mid-Market coverage with a longer cycle and multi-threading requirements; a packaging or pricing change; or deprioritizing the segment.')

p('**Priority 4: Q3 diagnostic before FY27 planning locks.** Owner: Will Ritchings (Revenue Operations); readout by October 31, 2026.')
p('*Prediction:* We identify the dominant cause of the Q3 break with enough confidence to correct FY27 capacity and quota assumptions.')
p('*Plan:*')
b('Compare pre- and post-migration deal cohorts on stage progression and SDR process.')
b('Audit the July 10–17 close-out window.')
b('Review notes on the 97 Q3 lost opportunities that reached Discovery.')
b('Check the pricing and packaging change log.')
b('Compare the new SDR cohort with tenured SDRs once their sample allows it.')

p('**Priority 5: Make loss data trustworthy.** Owner: Revenue Operations, with Emma Kyber; changes live by November 30, 2026.')
p('*Prediction:* Competitor capture reaches 90% and updated amounts reach 75% of deals past Intro (Goal 6). The next refresh of this analysis can then answer questions this one could only flag.')
p('*Plan:*')
b('Loss notes: required on every closed-lost deal.')
b('Competitor field: a required competitor or incumbent field when the reason is Competitor, Price Conscious, or Product Gaps.')
b('Picklist: new values for "No problem to solve" and "Wrong decision-maker" to break up Other.')
b('Amount: a required update at Business Case.')
b('Stale deals: a 30-day no-activity close-out rule for Discovery and Business Case.')
b('SDR ownership: consolidated into one field after a reviewed backfill. Any report that reads SDR ownership, including the weekly GTM dashboard and the SDR commission check in the pipeline hygiene agent, is audited for the same error.')
b('Retired fields: eight duplicate or empty deal fields left over from the Salesforce migration.')
b('Definitions: win rate, loss reason, funnel tier, and the SDR ownership rule documented in the Notion Data Definitions page.')
p('**Expected impact and how we will track it.** If Priorities 1 and 2 reach their targets and downstream conversion holds, the win rate rises to about 12.4%. At the trailing year\'s volume of 845 closed deals, that is roughly 105 wins instead of 74. Revenue Operations will report the six goal metrics weekly in the GTM dashboard, and refresh this analysis quarterly using the same workbook and field decisions so the results are comparable period over period. The KPI scorecard in Exhibit 10 lists every metric with its baseline, target, owner, and review cadence.')

